size for O, say 128 or 256.\n    # The kernel will compute the output for a block of O features for all B samples.\n    \n    # Let's try to process O in blocks of 256.\n    O_BLOCK_SIZE = 256\n    \n    # The kernel computes the output for a block of O features.\n    def kernel(x_block, weight_block, bias_block, output_block):\n        # x_block: (B, I)\n        # weight_block: (I, O_BLOCK_SIZE)\n        # bias_block: (O_BLOCK_SIZE,)\n        \n        # Matmul + Bias\n        # (B, I) @ (I, O_BLOCK_SIZE) + (O_BLOCK_SIZE,) -> (B, O_BLOCK_SIZE)\n        matmul_out = jnp.dot(x_block, weight_block) + bias_block\n        \n        # Mish 1\n        mish1 = matmul_out * jnp.tanh(jax.nn.softplus(matmul_out))\n        \n        # Mish 2\n        mish2 = mish1 * jnp.tanh(jax.nn.softplus(mish1))\n        \n        return mish2\n\n    # Pallas call setup\n    # We want to map the kernel over the output dimension O.\n    # The input dimensions are B (batch) and I (input features).\n    # The output dimension is O (output features).\n    \n    # We need to split weight (I, O) and bias (O,) along the O dimension.\n    \n    # The kernel takes:\n    # 1. x_block: (B, I) - Broadcast across O_BLOCK_SIZE\n    # 2. weight_block: (I, O_BLOCK_SIZE)\n    # 3. bias_block: (O_BLOCK_SIZE,)\n    # 4. output_block: (B, O_BLOCK_SIZE) - Accumulation target\n    \n    # Since the operation is not an accumulation (it's a direct computation),\n    # we can use the output block as the direct result.\n    \n    # We need to reshape inputs to fit the block structure.\n    # x needs to be broadcastable to (B, I, O_BLOCK_SIZE)\n    # weight needs to be split into (I, O_BLOCK_SIZE) chunks.\n    # bias needs to be split into (O_BLOCK_SIZE,) chunks.\n    \n    # We use pl.pallas_call to handle the loop over O.\n    \n    # The input x is (B, I). We treat it as (B, I, 1) for broadcasting.\n    # The weight is (I, O). We split it into O_BLOCK_SIZE chunks along O.\n    # The bias is (O,). We split it into O_BLOCK_SIZE chunks along O.\n    \n    # The output is (B, O). We will compute it in chunks of O_BLOCK_SIZE.\n    \n    # We use a loop over the output dimension O.\n    \n    # The kernel signature for pl.pallas_call is:\n    # kernel(input_slices, output_slices)\n    \n    # Input slices:\n    # x_slice: (B, I) -> Broadcast to (B, I, O_BLOCK_SIZE)\n    # weight_slice: (I, O_BLOCK_SIZE)\n    # bias_slice: (O_BLOCK_SIZE,)\n    \n    # Output slice:\n    # output_slice: (B, O_BLOCK_SIZE)\n    \n    # We need to reshape x to (B, I) and let Pallas handle the broadcasting over the block dimension.\n    \n    # The kernel implementation above is already structured for a single block computation.\n    # We use pl.pallas_call to iterate over the output dimension O.\n    \n    # The input x is (B, I). We need to pass it such that it is broadcast across the block dimension.\n    # The weight is (I, O). We split it into O_BLOCK_SIZE chunks.\n    # The bias is (O,). We split it into O_BLOCK_SIZE chunks.\n    \n    # We use jax.vmap or pl.pallas_call with appropriate shapes.\n    \n    # Since the operation is not a reduction, we can use pl.pallas_call to map the kernel over the output dimension.\n    \n    # The kernel signature for pl.pallas_call is:\n    # kernel(x_slice, weight_slice, bias_slice, output_slice)\n    \n    # We need to reshape x to (B, I) and let Pallas handle the broadcasting.\n    # We reshape weight to (I, O_BLOCK_SIZE) and bias to (O_BLOCK_SIZE,).\n    \n    # The output shape is (B, O). We will compute it in chunks of O_BLOCK_SIZE.\n    \n    # We use pl.pallas_call to iterate over the output dimension O.\n    \n    # The kernel signature for pl.pallas_call is:\n    # kernel(x_slice, weight_slice, bias_slice, output_slice)\n    \n    # We need to reshape x to (B, I) and let Pallas handle the broadcasting.\n    # We reshape weight to (I, O_BLOCK_SIZE) and bias to (O_BLOCK_SIZE,).\n    \n    # The output shape is (B, O). We will compute it in chunks of O_